feat: adopt the new design system — OKLCH palette, three fonts, tighter density

Ports the visual system from the design pass onto the working app. Because
everything already routed through CSS variables, the whole product recolours
from the token block.

Palette
- moved to OKLCH so both themes derive from the same hues instead of being
  eyeballed; light is no longer a rough inversion
- accent is now blue (hue 250) with amber (68) secondary, replacing the purple
  that every other indie dev tool also uses
- added translucent accent steps (--acc-a05..a60) and replaced 62 hardcoded
  purple literals across 20 files with tokens, so nothing is left stranded

Typography — three fonts, three jobs
- IBM Plex Sans for the interface (was: monospace forced on everything)
- JetBrains Mono kept for labels, counts and code
- Newsreader for prose, and serif is now the DEFAULT reading font rather than
  an opt-in; this is a writing app and prose reads better in a text face
- the reading-font picker still offers mono and sans

Density — matched to the design's measurements
- top bar 48 -> 40px with 24px controls, sidebar 240 -> 216px, tree rows
  26 -> 23px, rail 50 -> 52px

Notes
- Mermaid keeps literal hex, not tokens: it derives shades from these values
  and CSS variables break its colour maths
- the auth page was hardcoded dark; it now follows the theme
- verified the editor overlay still aligns under a proportional serif, which
  was the main risk in switching prose away from monospace
